---
// The website section of the privacy policy (src/pages/privacy.astro):
// server-side counters, private analytics, the optional opt-in analytics
// cookies (src/lib/consent.ts, host/consent.ts), the beta list, TestFlight,
// the hosted shell, and pairing links. Change LAST_UPDATED on the page with it.
---

<section aria-labelledby="website">
  <h2 id="website">the website</h2>
  <p>
    <code>phux.sh</code> and <code>docs.phux.sh</code> run on Cloudflare.
    The site loads no third-party analytics script and sets no analytics
    cookie unless you choose to allow one. Its fonts load from Bunny
    Fonts, which receives your IP address as part of that request. Two
    kinds of measurement always run on the server side, and a third runs
    only if you opt in.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Public aggregate counters.</strong> For each request, the site
    increments hourly counters by user-agent class (for example
    "browser" or a named AI crawler), country, hostname, response status,
    and type of request. Page views and installer requests also count an
    allowlisted <code>ref</code> tag and the referring site reduced to a
    channel name, never the full address. These counters contain no IP
    addresses, raw user agents, or cookies. They are published live at
    <a href="/telemetry">/telemetry</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Private analytics.</strong> Each request is also recorded in a
    private event store. It stores the hostname and page path (never the
    query string, except the campaign tags <code>utm_source</code>, <code>utm_medium</code>, and <code>utm_campaign</code>),
    the referring site's hostname, country, and user-agent class, and for
    installer downloads the operating system, CPU architecture, and
    download tool. Visitors are counted
    with an ID computed from your IP address and user agent under a key
    that changes every day, so visits cannot be linked from one day to
    the next. The IP address itself is never stored. To tell people apart
    from AI agents, crawlers, and scanners, a summary of a visitor's day
    (user-agent class, country, referrer type, campaign tags, and pages
    visited; for unrecognized clients, a truncated user-agent string with
    email addresses removed) can be sent to TypeSafe, an AI
    classification service. The summary never includes an IP address,
    email address, or cookie.
  </p>
  <p id="optional-analytics">
    <strong>Optional analytics (opt-in).</strong> On your first visit a
    small card asks whether you allow analytics. Until you choose, and if
    you choose "No thanks", nothing below happens and nothing is stored on
    your device. If you choose "Allow", the site sets two first-party
    cookies shared by <code>phux.sh</code> and <code>docs.phux.sh</code>:
    <code>phux_consent</code>, which remembers your choice, and
    <code>phux_vid</code>, a random ID that is not derived from anything
    about you. Both expire after 13 months. With that ID we can connect
    your visits across days, so we can see where you first came from
    (the referring site and campaign tags of the page where you allowed
    analytics), which pages you read, how long a page stayed visible,
    whether you copied an install command or clicked a download, and
    which outside sites you left for (GitHub, Discord, and so on, never
    the full address). This history is kept in the same private event
    store, never shared, and never combined with data from other
    companies. If your browser sends a
    <a href="https://globalprivacycontrol.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global Privacy Control</a>
    signal, we treat it as "No thanks" and do not ask. You can change your
    mind at any time with <button type="button" class="privacy-choices" data-consent-open>Privacy choices</button>
    (also in the footer). Withdrawing deletes the cookie and erases the
    history linked to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Download counters.</strong> To count downloads we also read
    the public totals that GitHub and npm publish for our releases and
    packages. These are counts, not information about individuals.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The beta list.</strong> If you <a href="/more#beta">join the beta</a>,
    we store your email address, the
    date you joined, the form you used, your country, and your user-agent
    class. We use it to email you about the beta. We do not sell or rent
    it. The beta emails include a personal link. If you choose to open it,
    it sets a first-party <code>phux_mid</code> cookie for one year, which
    links your later visits on that browser to your beta membership.
    Apart from the optional analytics cookie above, this cookie is the
    only way the site recognizes a returning visitor, and it is only set
    if you click that link.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>TestFlight requests.</strong> You can join through an enabled
    public TestFlight link without giving this site an email address; Apple
    handles joining and installation. If you use the email form to
    <a href="/more#testflight">request access to phux for iPhone</a>, we send
    your email address to Apple through App Store Connect, which adds you
    as a TestFlight tester and emails you the invite. Apple keeps tester
    records under its own privacy policy. If you ask for the Android beta
    instead, we add the address to the app's Google Play testing list by
    hand, and Google handles it under its own privacy policy. We also keep
    either request on the beta list described above. To leave the beta,
    tap Stop Testing in TestFlight, leave the test in Google Play, or email
    us and we remove you as a tester.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The hosted shell.</strong> The live terminal on the landing
    page runs on <code>shell.phux.sh</code>. The basic version needs no
    sign-in. To unlock the full Linux shell, you sign in with GitHub or
    Google. From GitHub we read your public user ID, username, and account
    creation date. From Google we read your account ID, name, and verified
    email address. Your identity is kept in a signed cookie that expires
    after 8 hours. On our side, only your provider account ID is kept, to
    enforce usage limits: launch records are deleted after one hour and
    daily usage totals at the end of the UTC day. Your IP address is used
    to rate-limit launches. It is stored in keyed-hash form for rate
    limiting, and in plain form only while a hosted session is running.
    Hosted sessions are discarded when they end.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pairing links.</strong> When a <code>phux.sh/connect</code> pairing link opens in a browser instead of the app, the page reads the
    link on your device and removes the token from the address bar.
    Links from current phux versions keep the token after the
    <code>#</code>, which browsers never send to our server. The page
    loads no analytics script, and our analytics record only the path,
    never the token.
  </p>
</section>
